  "shared_feature_description": "Both instances share the feature 'education-num', which is a categorical proxy for education level encoded as a numeric value. Despite its nominal categorization, it exhibits a spurious correlation with income due to historical data collection biases where higher education levels are overrepresented in higher-income groups.",
  "explanation": "The model has learned a spurious correlation between education-num and income, misinterpreting higher education as a guarantee of higher earnings. In Instance A, a low education-num (9, HS-grad) is incorrectly predicted as >50K, while in Instance B, a high education-num (13, Bachelors) is wrongly predicted as <=50K. This indicates the model relies on the feature's biased correlation rather than actual income drivers, leading to systematic misclassification.",
